  "titulo": "The clock that ran late",
  "ppm": 130,
  "texto": "# The workshop\n@energy 2\n> Narrate calmly, with a touch of mystery.\n\nDon Aurelio had a clock shop on a narrow street downtown.\nThe walls were covered with clocks of every size, and all of them showed the same time.\nPeople in the neighborhood said time never made mistakes in there.\nThat steady ticking was, to him, the most **beautiful** music in the world.\nOne rainy morning, a young woman named Ines came in with a wooden box under her arm.\nInside was a table clock, golden, with flowers painted on its face.\n\"It was my grandmother's,\" she explained. \"It works, but it always runs seven minutes slow.\"\nDon Aurelio examined it through his magnifying glass.\nIt had dust, a tired spring and a small crack in the glass.\n\"Leave it with me for a week. I'll make it **perfect**,\" he promised.\nInes hesitated before letting go of the box.\n[soft]It was as if she were saying goodbye to someone.[/soft]\n\n# The problem\n@energy 3\n> The tension builds: something doesn't make sense.\n\nDon Aurelio cleaned every piece, changed the spring and adjusted the wheels one by one.\nHe left it running all night beside the most accurate clock in the shop.\nThe next morning, Ines's clock was seven minutes slow.\n\"Impossible!\" the clockmaker muttered, taking off his glasses.\nHe repeated the work twice more, with the same **result**.\nIt wasn't six minutes, or eight: it was always exactly seven.\nHe no longer knew what else to check.\n[pause 2s]\nThen he noticed something he hadn't seen before.\nOn the back, hidden under a brass plate, there was a tiny **door**.\nWhat could a clock be hiding in a place like that? [up]\nWith a fine needle, he opened it very slowly.\n\n# The secret\n@energy 4\n> The climax: restrained emotion.\n\nInside was a small treasure: a folded piece of paper, yellow with age.\nHe sat down at his workbench and unfolded it with care.\nIt was a handwritten note, in elegant writing and almost faded ink.\n\"Rosa: I set this clock back so I could have seven more minutes with you every morning.\"\nBelow it was a signature and a date from sixty years earlier.\nAll at once, Don Aurelio understood.\nInes's grandfather had set the clock slow **on purpose**.\n[pause 2s]\nEvery morning, the clock told them it was still early, and they ate breakfast together without hurrying.\nHe arrived at work seven minutes late, but he gained seven minutes with her.\n\"What a **strange** deal, and what a beautiful one!\" he said out loud, alone in the shop.\n\n# The decision\n@energy 2\n> Intimate and warm. End slowly.\n\nA week later, Ines came back to the shop.\nDon Aurelio handed her the clock, clean and shiny, with new glass.\n\"I fixed it,\" he said. \"But first, I want to show you something.\"\nHe gave her the note and waited in silence while she read it.\nInes put a hand over her mouth.\n[soft]\"My grandmother always said my grandfather was the least punctual man in the world.\"[/soft]\nShe laughed through her tears.\n\"Would you like me to set it to the exact time?\" the clockmaker asked.\nInes thought for a moment and shook her head.\n\"No. Leave it with its seven **minutes**. They belong to them.\"\nSince then, Ines's house has a clock that runs seven minutes late, and nobody **minds**. [down]\n"
