  "titulo": "The dog who waited at the station",
  "ppm": 130,
  "texto": "# The station\n@energy 2\n> Narrate tenderly.\n\nAt the little station of San Lorenzo, the seven o'clock train always arrived on time.\nAnd always, on the platform, a dog the color of **cinnamon**, with one floppy ear, was waiting for it.\nNo one knew where he had come from.\nRafael, the stationmaster, called him Captain, because he seemed to inspect every car.\nHe saved him bread, a little rice and fresh water in an old dish.\nCaptain never got on the train.\nHe only wagged his tail when the doors opened, as if he were greeting each passenger.\nThe travelers got used to him.\nSome brought him cookies, which he ate with pleasure; others patted his head before boarding.\n\"Who are you waiting for, my friend?\" Rafael would ask him every morning.\nThe dog just looked at him with calm eyes, as if the answer were **obvious**.\n\n# The day of the fair\n@energy 3\n> The tension builds.\n\nOne spring Saturday, the train arrived full of families heading to the town fair.\nThere were balloons, music and a noise Captain had never heard before.\nIn the crowd, a five-year-old girl let go of her mother's hand to look at a **red** balloon.\nWhen she turned around, her mother was gone.\n[pause 2s]\nLucia started walking between legs and suitcases, farther and farther away.\nShe reached the end of the platform, where the roof ended and the tracks began.\nShe sat down on the edge and started to cry.\nOn the other side of the station, her mother was calling her name again and again.\n\"Lucia! Lucia!\"\nThe stationmaster dropped everything to look for her, but there were too many **people**.\n\n# Captain\n@energy 4\n> The climax: urgency and relief.\n\nThen Captain lifted his floppy ear and broke into a run.\nHe shot through the crowd like an arrow, dodging bags, wheels and shoes.\nHe found Lucia at the edge of the platform, just as a freight train was approaching.\nHe stood in front of her and barked once, **loud**.\nThe little girl jumped and stepped back toward the wall.\n[pause 2s]\nThe train roared past a few meters away.\nCaptain lay down beside her and rested his head on her knees.\nLucia stopped crying and hugged him.\nThat was how Rafael and her mother found them, a minute later.\n\"This dog is a **hero**!\" the woman said, her voice breaking.\nRafael smiled: \"I always knew he was waiting for something.\"\n\n# Back home\n@energy 2\n> Calm. End with a smile.\n\nLucia's family wanted to take Captain home with them.\nRafael agreed, although that night he missed his friend on the platform.\nBut the next morning, at seven o'clock sharp, something **curious** happened.\nDown the road from town came Lucia, with her school backpack, and Captain, wagging his tail.\nThe dog sat on the platform, as always, to wait for the train.\n[soft]When the doors opened, he greeted the passengers one by one.[/soft]\nThen he walked Lucia to school, crossing the tracks at the pedestrian crossing.\nSince that day, Captain has two jobs and two homes.\nIn the evenings, he sleeps beside Lucia's bed, with his floppy ear on the pillow.\nAnd Rafael no longer wonders who he's **waiting** for. [down]\n"
