  "titulo": "The girl who collected clouds",
  "ppm": 130,
  "texto": "# The jars\n@energy 2\n> A bedtime story voice, soft and warm.\n\nIn a village of yellow hills lived a girl named Amalia.\nShe had a collection that no one else in the world had: she kept **clouds**.\nShe caught them at dawn, when they came down to rest on the meadow.\nShe used glass jars, the kind her grandmother filled with jam.\nShe had round clouds, long clouds, a giant one and a tiny one shaped like a rabbit.\nEach jar was a small treasure.\nEvery night she lined them up on the shelf by her window.\n[soft]Sometimes, in the silence, one of them let three drops fall on the glass.[/soft]\nHer grandmother was the only one who never laughed at her.\nHer older brother, on the other hand, teased her all the time.\n\"What good is a cloud in a jar?\" he would ask.\nAmalia just shrugged.\n\"I don't know yet. But **someday** they'll be useful.\"\n\n# The drought\n@energy 3\n> The tension builds, without hurry.\n\nThat summer, it didn't rain a single time.\nThe river shrank to a trickle, and the cows looked for shade under the dry trees.\nEven the birds stopped singing in the afternoons.\nThe grown-ups looked at the sky every morning, their foreheads **wrinkled** with worry.\nIn the square, in front of the church, the mayor announced that the village well was almost empty.\n[pause 2s]\nThat night, Amalia couldn't sleep.\nHer brother wasn't teasing anymore: he was thirsty too.\nShe looked at her jars one by one, lit by the moon.\nThe clouds were stirring, as if they were thirsty as well.\nThen she understood **why** she had kept them for so long.\nShe put on her sneakers without a sound and slipped out of the house with the jars in her arms.\n\n# The rain\n@energy 4\n> The climax: growing joy.\n\nShe climbed the highest hill in the village in the middle of the night.\nShe set the jars on the grass and took a deep breath.\nThen she opened them one by one, very carefully.\nFor a moment, the clouds hesitated, as if they were afraid to leave her.\nThen they drifted out, stretching like cats after a nap.\nThey rose, gathered and grew until they covered the **whole** sky.\n[pause 2s]\nThe first drop landed on her nose.\nThen came another, and another, and suddenly it was raining over the whole valley.\nThe neighbors came out of their houses in pajamas, laughing and looking up.\n\"It's raining! It's finally raining!\" her brother shouted, soaking wet.\nAmalia raised her arms and let the water run down her **face**.\n\n# The last jar\n@energy 2\n> End very slowly, like the end of a fairy tale.\n\nIt rained for three days straight.\nThe river sang again, the well filled up and the fields turned green once more.\nThe cows went back to the fields, and the mayor gave a speech that nobody listened to.\nNo one could explain where those clouds had come from.\nWell, almost no one.\nHer brother gave her a new jar, the biggest one he could find in the kitchen.\n\"So you can start a new collection,\" he said, a little embarrassed.\nAmalia put it on the shelf, empty and shining.\n[soft]Every morning at dawn, she looks at it with patience, waiting for the **right** cloud.[/soft]\nThey say that if you watch that window with care, you'll sometimes see her smile.\nAnd in that village, ever since, no one laughs at a little girl's **dreams**. [down]\n"
